 {:finding :no-confirmed-universal-distributed-atomicity-demand
  :interpretation "Lines 145 and 154 request an owner-defined execution/output fence and correctly reject check-then-remote-call races. They need not mean instantaneous revocation everywhere or retracting already emitted bytes; do not introduce either as a new S4A gate."
  :required-owner-decision "Before any positive runtime qualification, the actual authority and dispatcher owners must explicitly define the finite admission/commit and read-release boundaries, expiry clock semantics, and ordering of revocation versus consumption. Revoke acknowledged before commit denies; a concurrent revoke/commit must have one owner-defined ordering. Final native identity/revision/request/scope/consent validation and one-use claim remain mandatory. A completed mutation is not undone by a later revoke, nor can already delivered bytes be recalled."
  :bounded-practical-case "Qualify one nominated endpoint, one dispatcher epoch, a bounded owner-approved operation grant and serialized native execution/release fence. Use expiry and owner-fence invalidation with fail-closed disconnect/outage handling. If remote revocation propagation or an in-flight grant has a nonzero validity window, its exact semantics need explicit owner/parent admission, not an adapter's silent cached-allow or an assumption that TTL equals immediate revocation. No such window is authorized by this review."
  :limits "No universal distributed transaction is required merely to implement offline source. If the actual owner protocol cannot meet the admitted boundary, keep real positives held. This is an owner engineering/admission decision, not a request that the user rediscover APIs or relax the card."}
